OSKit NIC server — virtio-net front door (kernel-thread first cut)
===================================================================

Minix 3-style server that fronts the single virtio-net driver.
Clients go through IPC; the driver is no longer a grab-it-
yourself kernel symbol. Phase 13's end state is for this server
to run as an isolated boot module with a kernel-granted MMIO
mapping + DMA-capable page grants; for now it runs as a kernel
thread in the driver's address space so we can nail the IPC
shape without needing new VM plumbing.

Coexists with `oskit/drivers/virtio/net_handler.lsysl` — which
still owns the ARP + ICMP echo demo — by splitting
responsibilities: net_handler does the RX poll / ARP / ICMP
round-trip at boot, and nic_server handles TX + MAC queries
from clients. The two do not share any mutable driver state
except the underlying split virtqueues, and for phase 13 the
driver's send/recv paths are called serially (no overlap) by
design. Once inet becomes a proper RX subscriber, the
net_handler shim goes away and nic_server owns everything.

IPC protocol
------------

Each request carries a 1-byte command code in `buf[0]`. Replies
always start with a 1-byte status (0 on success, -1 on error).

- GET_MAC: `cmd` → `status, mac(6)`
- SEND_PACKET: `cmd, len(2, big-endian), frame(len)` → `status`
- SUBSCRIBE_RX: `cmd` → `status`
    Registers the calling thread as the single RX notification
    target. On each frame the poller (currently `net_thread` in
    net_handler.lsysl) calls `nic_deliver_rx(frame, len)`, which
    enqueues the frame into the nic's 8-slot RX queue and sends
    a `notify_send_to(subscriber_tid, 1)` ping so the subscriber
    can drain the queue.
- RECV_PACKET: `cmd` → `status, len(2, big-endian), frame(len)`
    Non-blocking fetch of the head of the RX queue. `status = 0`
    + the frame on success; `status = -1` + `len = 0` when the
    queue is empty.

All multi-byte wire fields are big-endian to match the rest of
the stack. Unknown commands reply `status = -1`.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{port_create, port_register, ipc_recv, ipc_reply}
    import oskit.services.{notify_send_to, sleep, yield}
    import oskit.drivers.virtio.{virtio_net_get_mac, virtio_net_send,
        virtio_net_recv, net_send_arp_request, net_send_icmp_echo,
        net_handle_frame, net_gw_mac_known,
        NET_GW_IP_A, NET_GW_IP_B, NET_GW_IP_C, NET_GW_IP_D}


Command codes (byte value of buf[0]).

    val NIC_CMD_GET_MAC      = 1
    val NIC_CMD_SEND_PACKET  = 2
    val NIC_CMD_SUBSCRIBE_RX = 3
    val NIC_CMD_RECV_PACKET  = 4


Capacity. Ethernet II frames without VLAN cap at 1514 bytes; the
message buffer carries that plus the 3-byte header (cmd + u16
len) plus a little slop.

    val NIC_MAX_FRAME = 1514
    val NIC_MSG_BUF   = 1520


RX queue — fixed-size circular buffer. Eight slots is plenty for
bring-up (QEMU's user stack sends handfuls of frames per demo);
the size is constrained by the module-level storage budget more
than by any protocol concern.

    val NIC_RX_QUEUE = 8

    struct NicRxFrame
        data: [1514]byte
        len: int

    var nic_rx_queue: [8]NicRxFrame
    var nic_rx_head = 0
    var nic_rx_tail = 0
    var nic_rx_count = 0
    var nic_rx_drops = 0


Notification subscriber. Only one subscriber today — inet will
be the sole consumer once ARP/ICMP dispatch moves there. A full
pub/sub list is a later refinement.

    var nic_subscriber_tid = -1


Cached port, set once in `nic_server`.

    var nic_port = -1


### Byte-buffer helpers

Match the rest of the stack — big-endian u16 read out of an
arbitrary byte offset, no alignment assumptions.

    nic_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)


### GET_MAC handler

Reply: `[status(1), mac(6)]`. Returns `status = -1` and a zero
MAC if the virtio-net device has no MAC configured (shouldn't
happen under QEMU with `-device virtio-net-device,mac=...`, but
we don't want to trust that invariant unconditionally).

    nic_handle_get_mac(sender: int)
        var reply: [7]byte
        var mac: [6]byte
        if virtio_net_get_mac(mac) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            var i = 0
            while i < 6
                reply[1 + i] = 0
                i += 1
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < 6
            reply[1 + i] = mac[i]
            i += 1
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### SEND_PACKET handler

Expects a raw Ethernet frame including the 14-byte L2 header
but WITHOUT the 12-byte virtio-net header (the driver prepends
that internally). Returns status 0 on success, -1 if the length
is out of range or the driver rejects the frame.

    nic_handle_send_packet(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val len = nic_read_u16(buf, 1)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if len <= 0 || len > NIC_MAX_FRAME
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val frame: *byte = buf + 3
        val r = virtio_net_send(frame, len)
        if r == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else
            reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### SUBSCRIBE_RX handler

Record the sender's thread ID as the sole RX notification
target. Subsequent `nic_deliver_rx` calls from the poller will
wake this thread via `notify_send_to` so it can drain the queue
with RECV_PACKET.

    nic_handle_subscribe_rx(sender: int)
        nic_subscriber_tid = sender
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### RECV_PACKET handler

Pop one frame off the head of the RX queue. Non-blocking: when
the queue is empty we reply with `status = -1` and `len = 0`
rather than deferring, so clients can poll freely without
worrying about leaking waiters in the nic.

    nic_handle_recv_packet(sender: int)
        if nic_rx_count <= 0
            var empty: [3]byte
            empty[0] = byte(-1)
            empty[1] = 0
            empty[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, empty, 3)
            return
        val slot = nic_rx_head
        val len = nic_rx_queue[slot].len
        var reply: [1517]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((len >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte(len & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < len
            reply[3 + i] = nic_rx_queue[slot].data[i]
            i += 1
        nic_rx_head = (slot + 1) % NIC_RX_QUEUE
        nic_rx_count -= 1
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3 + len)


### nic_deliver_rx

Called from whichever thread is currently draining
`virtio_net_recv` (for this phase that's `net_thread` in
`oskit/drivers/virtio/net_handler.lsysl`). Enqueues `frame`
into the internal queue and, if a subscriber is registered,
sends them a 1-valued notification so they know to RECV_PACKET.

On overflow the frame is dropped and `nic_rx_drops` is bumped —
UDP-ish semantics. We do not notify on drop, since the queue
was already full and the subscriber presumably has something
to drain.

    nic_deliver_rx(frame: *byte, len: int)
        if len <= 0 || len > NIC_MAX_FRAME
            return
        if nic_rx_count >= NIC_RX_QUEUE
            nic_rx_drops += 1
            return
        val slot = nic_rx_tail
        nic_rx_queue[slot].len = len
        var i = 0
        while i < len
            nic_rx_queue[slot].data[i] = frame[i]
            i += 1
        nic_rx_tail = (slot + 1) % NIC_RX_QUEUE
        nic_rx_count += 1
        if nic_subscriber_tid >= 0
            notify_send_to(nic_subscriber_tid, 1)


### nic_poller

Kernel-thread entry point that owns the RX side of the driver.
Replaces the old `net_thread` from `oskit/drivers/virtio/
net_handler.lsysl` — we moved the loop here so `nic.lsysl` is
the single consumer of `virtio_net_recv`, and so ownership of
the RX path matches the architectural picture even while nic
is still a kernel thread.

At boot the poller still drives the ARP + ICMP echo demo that
proved TX/RX end-to-end (send one ARP request for the gateway;
once the reply arrives, send one ICMP echo). Every frame passes
through both `net_handle_frame` (ARP / ICMP responder) **and**
`nic_deliver_rx` (enqueue for subscribers). The demo is a
temporary shim — once inet becomes the RX subscriber and owns
ARP/ICMP, the `net_handle_frame` call goes away and this loop
shrinks to "recv → deliver → yield".

    nic_poller()
        var our_mac: [6]byte
        if virtio_net_get_mac(our_mac) == 0
            return
        sleep(10)
        net_send_arp_request(our_mac,
            NET_GW_IP_A, NET_GW_IP_B, NET_GW_IP_C, NET_GW_IP_D)

        var buf: [1514]byte
        var ping_sent = 0
        while true
            val n = virtio_net_recv(buf, 1514)
            if n > 0
                net_handle_frame(our_mac, buf, n)
                nic_deliver_rx(buf, n)
            else
                if ping_sent == 0 && net_gw_mac_known != 0
                    net_send_icmp_echo(our_mac, 0x1234, 1)
                    ping_sent = 1
                yield()


### Server main loop

Kernel-thread entry point. Spawned from `kernel_main_rs` in
`hello.lsysl` on aarch64. Creates port "nic", registers the
name so clients can look it up, and blocks on ipc_recv for each
request.

`ipc_recv` returning a negative sender is only possible on
shutdown; we just loop back and try again in that case (kernel
threads don't have a clean "exit" path in SLIX yet).

    nic_server()
        nic_port = port_create()
        port_register(nic_port, "nic")

        var buf: [1520]byte
        while 1 == 1
            val sender = ipc_recv(nic_port, buf, NIC_MSG_BUF)
            if sender >= 0
                val cmd = int(buf[0]) & 0xff
                if cmd == NIC_CMD_GET_MAC
                    nic_handle_get_mac(sender)
                else if cmd == NIC_CMD_SEND_PACKET
                    nic_handle_send_packet(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == NIC_CMD_SUBSCRIBE_RX
                    nic_handle_subscribe_rx(sender)
                else if cmd == NIC_CMD_RECV_PACKET
                    nic_handle_recv_packet(sender)
                else
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    reply[0] = byte(-1)
                    ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
